<article class="env-definition" id="00ZY">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00ZY">Definition <span data-tag="00ZY">12.3.1</span>.</a> A category $\mathcal{A}$ is called <em>preadditive</em> if each morphism set $\mathop{\mathrm{Mor}}\nolimits _\mathcal {A}(x, y)$ is endowed with the structure of an abelian group such that the compositions </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \mathop{\mathrm{Mor}}\nolimits (x, y) \times \mathop{\mathrm{Mor}}\nolimits (y, z) \longrightarrow \mathop{\mathrm{Mor}}\nolimits (x, z)  \]
</div>
  <p> are bilinear. A functor $F : \mathcal{A} \to \mathcal{B}$ of preadditive categories is called <em>additive</em> if and only if $F : \mathop{\mathrm{Mor}}\nolimits (x, y) \to \mathop{\mathrm{Mor}}\nolimits (F(x), F(y))$ is a homomorphism of abelian groups for all $x, y \in \mathop{\mathrm{Ob}}\nolimits (\mathcal{A})$. </p>
</article>